  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Palantir Usg has $1.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 49 awards, with an average award size of $21.6M and obligations up 42.2% year over year. Obligations climbed from $67.6M in FY21 to $1.1B in FY26, with intermediate totals of $198M in FY22, $151M in FY23, $288M in FY24, and $745M in FY25. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $1B in FY2026 to date. Other buying agencies include the Department of State with $18M, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $12.1M, and the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation with $8.1M. The largest NAICS markets for obligations are Custom Computer Programming Services at $912M and Software Publishers at $124M, followed by Other Management Consulting Services at $22M. Smaller recorded markets include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $247K and an additional Software Publishers entry at $236K. Current-year totals are fiscal year to date and represent partial-year obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Palantir Usg’s FY2026 federal business changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are up 42.2% year over year. Fiscal-year totals rose to $1.1B in FY26 from $745M in FY25 and earlier totals of $288M in FY24, $151M in FY23, $198M in FY22, and $67.6M in FY21. The Department of Defense accounted for most FY2026 obligations with $1B. The average award size in FY2026 to date is $21.6M across 49 awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who buys Palantir Usg’s products and which markets generate the most obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $1B in FY2026 to date. Other buyers include the Department of State with $18M, NASA with $12.1M, and the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation with $8.1M. The top NAICS markets by obligations are Custom Computer Programming Services at $912M and Software Publishers at $124M, with Other Management Consulting Services at $22M."
      }
